  unvaccinated population is like running  out of susceptible people  who haven't been infected and they were  mainly the younger ones  and we're getting back this this larger  group of people have been vaccinated  which small proportion  are getting a mild disease and  this means that  the idea that vaccination is a complete  cure if you like for the diseases has to  be reevaluated  having said that it's much milder and we  have the other problem of  long covered and that is still in the  unvaccinated quite a  significant proportion of young people  about one percent  are getting debilitating symptoms at  three months that prevent them  going back to the normal function and  we're seeing  about 500 to 700 estimated cases a day  of that and that should be the parameter  that should be discussed  by the media not really deaths anymore  is the reason that you think we don't  need to focus on the death metric as  much now because you think that it's  going to be  uh one that stays low you think that and  that aspect of the pandemic sadly you  know the worst  outcome that you would die from this  disease is one that is now more or less  under control  i do believe that yes i think deaths are  not going to go suddenly out of control  um there might be some other  deaths as we go into winter again as we  combine with flu  but i don't think this is going to be  worse than you know a bad flu winter  rather than anything else so as long as  we have a a plan  to keep booster vaccinations going  in the very vulnerable and that  occurs well and there's no major shifts  in the in the variants  then i'm i'm not very worried about  deaths as the main  outcome and you're talking about  long coverage uh you know it's it's a  very frightening idea that one would be  debilitated  from having this virus for 10 days two  weeks and then you know potentially have  months if not a  lifetime of consequences of that  but one of the problems is we just still  don't know a lot about long cove 