  and  we need to many more resources than we  are putting into it at the moment  well we already have uh five million  people on waiting lists the new health  secretary sachi jabba thinks it could go  up to 13 million in the coming months  so certainly something that could  burden the health service further if we  take all of this into account especially  looking at what you call the new phase  taking into account  uh long covet as well what do you make  of the reopening that's been announced  on the 19th of july are you broadly in  favor of it or do you think that it's  too carefree i'm broadly in favor  i think there has to come a time when  you realize you can't keep people locked  up  and you can't keep telling everyone what  to do  and constraining their their liberties  and you have to balance that  the opening up with the benefits the  economy  which has major health impacts and of  course  uh try and encourage people who have  preventable diseases from  not just staying at home with them but  actually coming and up to hospitals and  feeling that  they're safer to come out and get  treated  because um you know the the the  collateral damage from people not  getting  cancer treatments or getting diagnosed  are horrendous really because already we  had won the worst records in europe for  cancer and this is just going to make it  worse so  also realizing that with the phase of  the i think it signals  definite change in the phase of the  disease as we move  to pandemic to endemic  and there'll still be lots of disease  going around we're never going to get  zero covered  and we are moving to milder disease as  long as  people get vaccinated so i think that's  an  important message that this uh  these lifting restrictions which have  been gradual people seem to think it's  all happening on one day but it's not  really  the google mobility data show that  you know people are back at about 85  percent of  uh normal anyway already now  so it's not like it's going 